## Hypothesis ID and title

**H-001 — Semantic HTML vs. Generic DOM structures**

## Research question

How does semantic HTML influence the extraction and interpretation of web content compared to generic DOM structures?

## Hypothesis statement

For equivalent content and presentation, semantic HTML5 may improve the accuracy of content extraction or structural interpretation compared with generic `div`-based markup.

## Theoretical background

HTML elements can express document roles and relationships in addition to containing text. Whether a target extractor or generative retrieval pipeline uses those signals, and whether they change outcomes, is an empirical question.

## Supporting literature

Not yet assessed. Add verified paper IDs from the literature register.

## Existing evidence

No evidence recorded; the statement is a testable proposal, not a finding.

## Expected outcome

If supported in the tested setup, semantic variants will score better on predeclared extraction and structural interpretation measures than the content-equivalent generic variant.

## Alternative explanations and potential counterarguments

- Extractors may rely primarily on text, accessibility trees, learned parsing, or other signals.
- HTML semantics may not be retained by downstream parsers.
- Differences may result from DOM size, nesting, text order, styling, or accessibility attributes rather than element semantics.
- A measured extraction gain may not transfer to retrieval or generated answers.

## Validation approach

Use the content-equivalent variants in [EXP-001](/en/05-experiments/exp-001/), predefine annotations and metrics, repeat runs where applicable, and report each pipeline stage separately.

